"""
Replays a recorded input sequence, one sample per tick, from inside the synchronous
program.

The sample comes from `traj_value`, a call out to C (`csrc/qube_traj.c`, via
`src/traj_source.jl`), so the same component replays the file on the Julia backend, the
`:c` backend and exported standalone C alike — the mirror of what `DataLogger` does for
the rows leaving the program. `filename` and `column` are **structural** parameters,
i.e. build-time Julia values that never enter the equations: a file name cannot cross a
synchronous node's interface, so the driver opens the file (`open_traj!`) with the
values the model was built with.

`k` is the program's own tick counter, and it is what indexes the trajectory. Counting
ticks rather than measuring time is deliberate: the sequence was designed for a fixed
sample rate, so sample *k* belongs to tick *k* whatever the loop's jitter did, and a
missed deadline shifts the whole replay rather than skipping a sample.

Past the end of the file `u` is 0, so a run longer than the trajectory coasts instead
of holding the last value. With no trajectory open the component is inert and outputs
0, which is what lets a model containing one be built and stepped without any file.

Clock-agnostic.
"""
component TrajectorySource@[input clk extends Discrete]
  "Sample for this tick, 0 past the end of the trajectory"
  u = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^u}
  "Tick counter, 1 on the first tick; the index into the trajectory"
  k = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^k}
  "File the samples are read from, opened by the driver with this name"
  structural parameter filename::String = ""
  "Column of the file to read, 1-based"
  structural parameter column::Integer = 2
relations
  initial k@(clk-1) = 0
  k@clk = k@(clk-1) + 1
  u@clk = traj_value(k)
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/TrajectorySource.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "u": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "k": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 600, "y2": 700}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Lets a commanded voltage through while the arm stays where it should, and takes over
when it does not.

An open-loop replay has nothing holding the arm: the designed sequence keeps it inside
its range under the models it was designed for, and this exists for the case where the
device disagrees with all of them. Three layers, in order:

  * beyond `warn`, the command is replaced by `-pullback * arm`, steering the arm back
    towards centre instead of continuing the replay;
  * beyond `abort`, the supervisor **latches** and the command is 0 for the rest of the
    run. Latched rather than momentary because an arm that has reached the abort angle
    has already shown that the replay is not safe on this device;
  * everything is clamped to `±umax`, including the pull-back.

The latch is why this is a component and not an expression: it needs the previous tick's
state. Note what it replaces — the driver used to `break` out of its loop on the abort
condition, which left the last commanded voltage on the motor until the loop's cleanup
ran. Here the program itself writes 0 and keeps ticking, so the log records the whole
run including what the arm did afterwards.

`tripped` is 1 once the latch has fired, 0 before, so it can be logged.

Clock-agnostic.
"""
component SafetySupervisor@[input clk extends Discrete]
  "Commanded voltage, e.g. from a trajectory replay"
  u_des = Dyad.RealInput@[clk]() {^u_des}
  "Measured arm angle [rad], 0 centred"
  arm = Dyad.RealInput@[clk]() {^arm}
  "Voltage to write"
  u = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^u}
  "1 once the abort latch has fired, 0 before"
  tripped = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^tripped}
  "Hard clamp on anything written [V]"
  parameter umax::Dyad.Voltage = 3.0
  "Arm angle beyond which the command is replaced by a pull-back [rad]"
  parameter warn::Dyad.Angle = 1.6580627893946132
  "Arm angle beyond which the supervisor latches and commands 0 [rad]"
  parameter abort::Dyad.Angle = 2.0943951023931953
  "Pull-back gain past `warn` [V/rad]"
  parameter pullback::Real = 2.0
relations
  initial tripped@(clk-1) = 0
  tripped@clk = ifelse(abs(arm) > abort, 1.0, tripped@(clk-1))
  u@clk = ifelse(tripped > 0.5, 0.0, clamp(ifelse(abs(arm) > warn, -pullback * arm, u_des), -umax, umax))
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/SafetySupervisor.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "u_des": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "arm": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 600, "y2": 700}}}
    },
    "u": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 300, "y2": 400}}}
    },
    "tripped": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 600, "y2": 700}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Open-loop replay of a designed input sequence on the physical QUBE, as a single
synchronous program.

The point is plain system identification: drive the device with a known input and record
what it did, so the model's parameters can be fitted to the pair —
examples/pendulum_identification.jl does the fitting. The input is a designed sequence
rather than a step or a chirp because examples/input_design.jl optimizes it for how much
it says about the parameters, subject to keeping the arm inside its range; nothing here
depends on how the file was produced, though, and any recorded sequence replays the same
way. Everything is inside the program — `TrajectorySource` reads the sample, `SafetySupervisor` decides what
is safe to write, `HardwareMeasurement` reads the encoders, `HardwareCommand` writes the
motor, `DataLogger` writes the row — so the driver supplies nothing but a clock tick, and
the same program runs in-process or as exported C on the machine the QUBE is attached to.

The log's first four columns are `time, shoulder_angle, elbow_angle, control_input`, which
is what examples/pendulum_identification.jl reads positionally (and
examples/analyze_discrimination.jl, which asks whether a run separates two candidate
parameter sets); `u_des`, the supervisor's latch and the loop diagnostics follow, so the log
says for itself where the supervisor intervened.

There is no feedback here beyond the supervisor: the arm has no restoring force, and a
replay that goes wrong goes wrong quickly. Keep `umax` low (3 V by default, against the
amplifier's 10) and stay near the machine.
"""
component FurutaIdentification
  measurement = HardwareMeasurement() {^measurement}
  trajectory = TrajectorySource(filename = traj_file, column = traj_column) {^trajectory}
  supervisor = SafetySupervisor(final umax = umax, final warn = warn, final abort = abort, final pullback = pullback) {^supervisor}
  command = HardwareCommand(final umax = umax) {^command}
  diagnostics = HardwareDiagnostics() {^diagnostics}
  logger = DataLogger(n = IDENTIFICATION_LOG_NCOLS, filename = log_file, header = IDENTIFICATION_LOG_HEADER) {^logger}
  periodicclock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(dt = Ts) {^periodicclock}
  "Sample time [s]; must match the rate the trajectory was designed for"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.005
  "File the designed input sequence is read from; the driver opens it with this name"
  structural parameter traj_file::String = IDENTIFICATION_TRAJ_FILE
  "Column of `traj_file` holding the voltage, 1-based"
  structural parameter traj_column::Integer = 2
  "File the log is written to; the driver opens it with this name"
  structural parameter log_file::String = IDENTIFICATION_LOG_FILE
  """
  Hard clamp on anything written [V]. Deliberately well below the amplifier's range: this
  is an open-loop replay, so nothing here corrects an unexpected response
  """
  parameter umax::Dyad.Voltage = 3.0
  "Arm angle beyond which the command is replaced by a pull-back [rad]"
  parameter warn::Dyad.Angle = 1.6580627893946132
  "Arm angle beyond which the supervisor latches and commands 0 [rad]"
  parameter abort::Dyad.Angle = 2.0943951023931953
  "Pull-back gain past `warn` [V/rad]"
  parameter pullback::Real = 2.0
relations
  connect(supervisor.u_des, logger.u[5], trajectory.u) {^id8}
  connect(measurement.elbow_angle, logger.u[3]) {^id9}
  connect(supervisor.u, command.u) {^id10}
  # The applied voltage is the diagnostics' dependency token as well as a logged column:
  # `exec` is latched by the write, so the reads have to be scheduled after it.
  connect(command.u_applied, diagnostics.dep, logger.u[4]) {^id11}
  connect(diagnostics.elapsed, logger.u[1])
  connect(supervisor.tripped, logger.u[6]) {^id12}
  connect(diagnostics.dt, logger.u[7])
  connect(diagnostics.exec, logger.u[8])
  connect(periodicclock.y, logger.u[2]) {^id13}
  connect(measurement.shoulder_angle, supervisor.arm, periodicclock.y) {^id14}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/FurutaIdentification.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "measurement": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 20, "x2": 120, "y1": 400, "y2": 500}}}
    },
    "trajectory": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 20, "x2": 120, "y1": 120, "y2": 220}}}
    },
